1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,790
Good morning,

2
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:06,543
and welcome to the museum - one with a remarkable range of exhibits,

3
00:00:06,543 --> 00:00:08,470
which I'm sure you'll enjoy.

4
00:00:08,470 --> 00:00:09,802
My name's Greg,

5
00:00:09,802 --> 00:00:13,070
and I'll tell you about the various collections as we go round.

6
00:00:13,070 --> 00:00:16,757
But before we go, let me just give you a taste of what we

7
00:00:16,757 --> 00:00:17,120
have here.

8
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:21,096
Well, for one thing, we have a fine collection of twentieth

9
00:00:21,096 --> 00:00:24,650
and twenty-first century paintings, many by very well-known artists.

10
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:27,070
I'm sure you'll recognise several of the paintings.

11
00:00:27,070 --> 00:00:30,866
This is the gallery that attracts the largest number of visitors,

12
00:00:30,866 --> 00:00:34,040
so it's best to go in early in the day, before the crowds arrive.

13
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:37,580
Then there are the nineteenth-century paintings.

14
00:00:37,580 --> 00:00:40,394
The museum was opened in the middle of that century,

15
00:00:40,394 --> 00:00:44,414
and several of the artists each donated one work - to get the museum

16
00:00:44,414 --> 00:00:45,540
started, as it were.

17
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:49,645
So they're of special interest to us - we feel closer to them than

18
00:00:49,645 --> 00:00:50,550
to other works.

19
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:53,906
The sculpture gallery has a number of fine exhibits,

20
00:00:53,906 --> 00:00:57,040
but I'm afraid it's currently closed for refurbishment.

21
00:00:57,040 --> 00:00:59,777
You'll need to come back next year to see it properly,

22
00:00:59,777 --> 00:01:03,238
but a number of the sculptures have been moved to other parts of the

23
00:01:03,238 --> 00:01:03,640
museum.

24
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:10,104
'Around the world' is a temporary exhibition - you've probably seen something about it on TV

25
00:01:10,104 --> 00:01:11,610
or in the newspapers.

26
00:01:11,610 --> 00:01:13,864
It's created a great deal of interest,

27
00:01:13,864 --> 00:01:18,170
because it presents objects from every continent and many countries,

28
00:01:18,170 --> 00:01:22,390
and provides information about their social context - why they were made,

29
00:01:22,390 --> 00:01:23,374
who for,

30
00:01:23,374 --> 00:01:24,660
and so on.

31
00:01:24,660 --> 00:01:27,350
Then there's the collection of coins.

32
00:01:27,350 --> 00:01:31,021
This is what you might call a focused, specialist collection,

33
00:01:31,021 --> 00:01:33,929
because all the coins come from this country,

34
00:01:33,929 --> 00:01:37,780
and were produced between two thousand and a thousand years ago.

35
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:42,383
And many of them were discovered by ordinary people digging their gardens,

36
00:01:42,383 --> 00:01:43,850
and donated to the museum!

37
00:01:43,850 --> 00:01:48,880
All our porcelain and glass was left to the museum by its founder,

38
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:50,530
when he died in 1878.

39
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:54,206
And in the terms of his will, we're not allowed to add anything to

40
00:01:54,206 --> 00:01:54,789
that collection:

41
00:01:54,789 --> 00:01:57,159
he believed it was perfect in itself,

42
00:01:57,159 --> 00:01:59,590
and we don't see any reason to disagree!

43
00:01:59,590 --> 00:02:03,105
OK,

44
00:02:03,105 --> 00:02:05,475
that was something about the collections,

45
00:02:05,475 --> 00:02:09,090
and now here's some more practical information, in case you need it.

46
00:02:09,090 --> 00:02:13,810
Most of the museum facilities are downstairs, in the basement,

47
00:02:13,810 --> 00:02:16,240
so you go down the stairs here.

48
00:02:16,240 --> 00:02:20,137
When you reach the bottom of the stairs, you'll find yourself in a sitting

49
00:02:20,137 --> 00:02:20,400
area,

50
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:25,524
with comfortable chairs and sofas where you can have a rest before continuing your

51
00:02:25,524 --> 00:02:26,770
exploration of the museum.

52
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:29,382
We have a very good restaurant,

53
00:02:29,382 --> 00:02:33,220
which serves excellent food all day, in a relaxing atmosphere.

54
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:34,604
To reach it,

55
00:02:34,604 --> 00:02:38,857
when you get to the bottom of the stairs, go straight ahead to the

56
00:02:38,857 --> 00:02:43,130
far side of the sitting area, then turn right into the corridor.

57
00:02:43,130 --> 00:02:46,330
You'll see the door of the restaurant facing you.

58
00:02:46,330 --> 00:02:49,005
If you just want a snack,

59
00:02:49,005 --> 00:02:53,027
or if you'd like to eat somewhere with facilities for children, we also have

60
00:02:53,027 --> 00:02:53,650
a cafe.

61
00:02:53,650 --> 00:02:58,088
When you reach the bottom of the stairs, you'll need to go straight ahead,

62
00:02:58,088 --> 00:03:00,377
turn right into the corridor,

63
00:03:00,377 --> 00:03:03,750
and the cafe is immediately on the right.

64
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:09,240
And talking about children, there are baby-changing facilities downstairs:

65
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:14,858
cross the sitting area, continue straight ahead along the corridor on the left,

66
00:03:14,858 --> 00:03:18,750
and you and your baby will find the facilities on the left-hand side.

67
00:03:18,750 --> 00:03:25,561
The cloakroom, where you should leave coats, umbrellas and any large bags, is on

68
00:03:25,561 --> 00:03:27,630
the left hand side of the sitting area.

69
00:03:27,630 --> 00:03:32,030
It's through the last door before you come to the corridor.

70
00:03:32,030 --> 00:03:34,480
There are toilets on every floor,

71
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:39,078
but in the basement they're the first rooms on the left when you get

72
00:03:39,078 --> 00:03:39,460
down there.

73
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:44,780
OK, now if you've got anything to leave in the cloakroom, please do that

74
00:03:44,780 --> 00:03:45,142
now,

75
00:03:45,142 --> 00:03:47,310
and then we'll start our tour.
